12|0@Portland^1438890071#ctty35p:ebmfreak$:If it is seriously bad enough where your neighbors have been threatened, I recommend you talk to the good folks at SE uplift: http://www.seuplift.org/ They have worked with PPD to assign neighborhood liason officers that take an active interest in SE - these are officers that also live in SE usually (so they are personally vested) We had a similar issue with a home in our neighborhood that went into a "trust" when the owner died - and her daughter moved in and turned it into a meth lab and drug-den. At the advice and help of the SE Uplift office -- we began working with neighbors on documenting, and recording of license plates... and creating a journal to hand off to the police. Once that happened, and we compiled a good amount of documentation, the SE Uplift was able to get the officers interested in fixing the issue. They were able to build a bigger case, and make multiple arrests etc. etc. and eventually the home was pulled from the trust (breaking usage rules) and forced to sale.
6|0@Portland^1438891132#cttyue5:Imperialgal$:Thats awesome, thanks for the link. We are interested in looking into that a bit further. A neighbor told me of another neighbor who has a confrontation on video. I think we should talk to the other neighbors who have had issues as well. 
4|0@Portland^1438896100#ctu2b11:warm_sweater$:You should also contact the Office for Neighborhood Involvement. They can help connect you with the community police officers in your area and other resources for helping with the problem.
3|0@Portland^1438892023#cttzh4x:ebmfreak$:Yeah - if your neighborhood is on this map - they should be able to help connect you to neighbors that'll be interested in stopping the activity: http://www.seuplift.org/connect-with-your-neighborhood/ 
